Resolves the "Module not found: Can't resolve '@0glabs/0g-serving-broker'" error that was preventing the web application from building successfully.

Problem

The Next.js build was failing with a webpack module resolution error when trying to import the @0glabs/0g-serving-broker SDK in web/lib/compute/clientBroker.ts. This was preventing the application from compiling and running.

Failed to compile.

./lib/compute/clientBroker.ts
Module not found: Can't resolve '@0glabs/0g-serving-broker'
Root Cause

The issue was caused by webpack's module resolution configuration not properly handling the SDK's dual CommonJS/ESM export structure. The SDK package exports both formats:

./lib.commonjs/index.js for Node.js/server environments
./lib.esm/index.mjs for browser/ESM environments
Solution

1. Fixed Webpack Configuration

Updated next.config.js to properly configure webpack's condition names for different environments:

Browser builds now use ['import', 'module', 'browser', 'default'] conditions to prefer ESM
Server builds use ['require', 'node', 'default'] conditions for CommonJS compatibility
2. Improved Error Handling

Enhanced the dynamic import in clientBroker.ts with better error handling and messaging when the SDK fails to load.

3. Fixed Dynamic Import

Added missing default export to ComputeProvider.tsx to resolve TypeScript errors with Next.js dynamic imports.

